{"builder":"Make one pull request that meets every acceptance line of https://github.com/o/r/issues/23:\n- math.js exports clamp(x, lo, hi), which returns x limited to the range lo to hi\n- clamp(x, lo, hi) throws a RangeError when lo is greater than hi\n- README.md documents every function math.js exports\n- npm test passes\n\nRepository facts: math.js (CommonJS, 'use strict') currently defines add, subtract and multiply and ends with `module.exports = { add, subtract, multiply };`. README.md has an `## API` section with `### add(a, b)` and `### multiply(a, b)` subsections, but subtract is NOT documented there yet. Tests live in test/math.test.js and use node:test with node:assert/strict; `npm test` runs `node --test`.\n\nSteps:\n1. In math.js, add a function `clamp(x, lo, hi)` with a short doc comment like the others. If `lo > hi`, throw `new RangeError(...)` with a clear message (check this before anything else). Otherwise return `Math.min(Math.max(x, lo), hi)`: x when lo <= x <= hi, lo when x < lo, hi when x > hi. Add clamp to the module.exports object, keeping add, subtract and multiply exported.\n2. In README.md, under `## API`, add a `### subtract(a, b)` subsection (returns `a` minus `b`, with a short example) and a `### clamp(x, lo, hi)` subsection that says it returns `x` limited to the range `lo` to `hi`, throws a RangeError when `lo` is greater than `hi`, and gives an example such as `clamp(-5, 0, 100); // 0` and `clamp(140, 0, 100); // 100`. Follow the existing heading and code-block style. Afterwards every name in math.js's module.exports must have its own `### name(...)` heading in the API section.\n3. In test/math.test.js, import clamp and add tests: value inside the range is returned unchanged (e.g. clamp(50, 0, 100) === 50), below range returns lo (clamp(-5, 0, 100) === 0), above range returns hi (clamp(140, 0, 100) === 100), boundaries (clamp(0, 0, 100) === 0, clamp(100, 0, 100) === 100), lo === hi works (clamp(7, 3, 3) === 3), and `assert.throws(() => clamp(1, 10, 0), RangeError)`.\n4. Run `npm test` and confirm all tests pass, then open the pull request referencing issue #23.","verifier":"Check the pull request head against each acceptance line of https://github.com/o/r/issues/23, in order. Run every command from the repository root; each command is self-contained.\n\nCheck 1 — \"math.js exports clamp(x, lo, hi), which returns x limited to the range lo to hi\"\nRun: node -e \"const m=require('./math.js'); console.log(typeof m.clamp); const c=m.clamp; console.log(JSON.stringify([c(50,0,100),c(-5,0,100),c(140,0,100),c(0,0,100),c(100,0,100),c(7,3,3),c(-3,-10,-1),c(2.5,1,2)]));\"\nPASS: the first line is `function` and the second line is `[50,0,100,0,100,3,-3,2]` (inside values unchanged, below-range values become lo, above-range values become hi).\nFAIL: clamp is not a function exported by math.js, the command errors, or any value differs from that list.\n\nCheck 2 — \"clamp(x, lo, hi) throws a RangeError when lo is greater than hi\"\nRun: node -e \"const {clamp}=require('./math.js'); for (const a of [[1,10,0],[5,2,1],[0,0.5,0.4]]) { try { clamp(...a); console.log('NO THROW', a); } catch (e) { console.log(e instanceof RangeError ? 'RangeError' : 'OTHER '+e); } }\"\nPASS: all three printed lines are `RangeError`.\nFAIL: any line prints `NO THROW` or `OTHER ...`.\n\nCheck 3 — \"README.md documents every function math.js exports\"\nRun: node -e \"const fs=require('fs'); const names=Object.keys(require('./math.js')).filter(k=>typeof require('./math.js')[k]==='function'); const r=fs.readFileSync('README.md','utf8'); for (const n of names) console.log(n, new RegExp('\\\\b'+n+'\\\\s*\\\\(').test(r) ? 'found' : 'MISSING');\"\nThen read README.md at the head and, for each exported name listed, confirm the README describes what that function does (not just mentions its name).\nPASS: every exported function (this should include add, subtract, multiply and clamp) prints `found` and the README has a description of each one.\nFAIL: any exported function prints `MISSING`, or a function is named in README.md without any description of what it does.\n\nCheck 4 — \"npm test passes\"\nRun: npm test\nPASS: the command exits with status 0 and reports no failing tests.\nFAIL: the command exits non-zero or reports any failing test.\n\nAdvisory (not pass/fail conditions): it is good if test/math.test.js adds tests for clamp, including a RangeError case, and if README.md's clamp entry mentions the RangeError and sits under the `## API` section with a `### clamp(x, lo, hi)` heading matching the existing style. Note that subtract was undocumented before this change, so check 3 needs the PR to document it."}